A||N||18||joeformula88||another one with hot starting problems||||1||
Z||000000||joeformula88||03-14-2000||04:25 AM||||I'm new to the forum, but have checked out every available message on problems with hot starting (cranks fine), but still would like some advice on this. My problem is accompanied by not responding properly when accelerating, the engine is stuttering a bit. Also the problem does not always appear (sometimes it starts fine when hot and doesn't stutter). I have a V6 '88 Formula manual speed, with approx. 60000 miles on it. New battery and Accel ign. coil are already in place. What's next?||||reg||1
Z||000001||RICK||03-14-2000||09:47 AM||||Joe..when you did the other replacements did you also replace the cap, roter, plugs, and wires?  When the car does not want to start check to see if your getting any spark.||||reg||1
Z||000002||joeformula88||03-14-2000||10:06 AM||||Rick, Thanks for the suggestions, but I wouldn't expect any ignition components to be the problem except when influenced by heat, since the car starts perfectly fine when cold.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||03-14-2000||10:21 AM||||The ignition module inside distributor can act up when hot. The pick-up coil insde distributor can act up. These are common distributor problems. The Fuel pump can over heat occasionally if it is starting to fail. These are just a couple of other things to check.||||reg||1
Z||000004||joeformula88||03-14-2000||10:38 AM||||Oreif, Thanks for the suggestions. Which is more likely to fail, the ignition module or pick-up coil or should I replace both at the same time. And can someone suggest where to buy these and if there are more reliable aftermarket-parts available. Thanks! <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||03-14-2000||03:28 PM||||Replace the coolant temperature sensor, Which may be what is causing your starting problems cold or hot. They are only $10, which is cheaper than replacing the ignition module. Some are even $8. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||03-14-2000||03:29 PM||||Replace the coolant temperature sensor, Which may be what is causing your starting problems cold or hot. They are only $10, which is cheaper than replacing the ignition module. Some are even $8. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||mwbackus||03-14-2000||08:13 PM||||May be oil pressure sender/switch or leaking fuel injector(s).  Watch oil pressure guage during hot starts and see if it acts strange.  Observe exhaust immediatly after hot start to determine if a rich condition (ie. leaking injector) could be the cause.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000009||joeformula88||03-15-2000||03:55 AM||||In the archives I found a guy named PATRICK with exactly the same problem, who was overloaded with suggestions, but never really let us know what finally cured the problem. So PATRICK, if you're still out there and you cured yours, please share the info...||||reg||4
Z||000010||Patrick||04-17-2000||06:37 PM||||<BR>Heh heh, this is pretty funny. I just did a <I>Search</I> for <I>hot start</I> problems and I came across this month old thread which I hadn't noticed before.<P>Well, I'm ashamed to admit it, but I never did anything about the hot start problems I had which first developed about a year ago. I sorta learned to live with it. (Sometimes I'm WAY too patient!) However, it's gotten worse and it's now to the point where I have no choice, something's got to be done. I'm getting tired of having to open my trunk lid to cool the engine almost every time I want to start a hot engine. There's a current thread running now which is discussing the same issue -<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/003205.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/003205.html</A> <P>It gets a bit confusing when ignition components are discussed because some of us have the DIS ignition (as I do) and it's not always clear (to me) if certain suggestions are for both types of ignition systems or not. Anyway, when I get this all sorted out, I'll post either here or in the other thread and say what cured my problem. That's assuming that I cure it... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000011||Patrick||04-18-2000||03:54 PM||||<BR>One thing that I don't think anyone has mentioned is whether the particular thermostat installed has any relevance to the <I>hot start</I> problem. As far as I know, there are two (stock) thermostats available for the 2.5L, 180 degrees and 195. (It might be the same for the 2.8L) Has anyone had any experience regarding whether or not it has made a difference which thermostat they had installed?<P>It seems to me that if an engine has a problem starting when it's hot, yet will start fine after the trunk has been open for five minutes, then it might make enough difference simply by replacing a 195 thermostat with a 180 unit. That 15 degree difference might make a world of difference.<P>I had changed mine from 180 to 195 three years ago when I bought my Fiero because I had previously used 195 degree thermostats in all my older high performance Chevys. However, I always felt that the Fiero didn't perform as well with this higher temperature thermostat. I believe Ogre has commented on this subject as well. Maybe it's been a HUGE mistake having the 195 thermostat installed. Not only does it appear to negatively affect performance, but it's possible that it also contributes to <I>hot start</I> problems.<P>I'm going to change the thermostat back to 180 and I'll report any improvement.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fformula88||04-18-2000||07:55 PM||||I have posted many ?'s on a mysterious running problem with my Formula... and not starting well when hot is a symptom.  It also idles irratically at times, hot or cold, and stalls when at stoplights now and then.  But often (sometimes lasting for weeks) it runs perfectly.  This "phantom" problem comes and goes, and is more annoying than anything.<P>I have replaced dist, wires, plugs, thermostat, and other tune up items.  When that didn't help, I took it in to the dealer (ack) and they searched it for vacumn leaks, replaced the and replaced the EGR tube.  They also checked the cat on my recomendation, and decided it was good.  <P>Under some recent advice in a thread, I took out and cleaned the IAC valve.  It still does not move in well, I can barely push it in by hand, but the car has ran better since.  It seems to start better when warm too.  Of course, this may be because the car is in a "good" time, and will revert to its poor running.  It may be worth checking anyways.||||reg||1
Z||000013||joeformula88||04-24-2000||03:06 PM||||Just two days ago I fixed my hot starting problem: Basically what caused it was a corroded connection between the pick-up coil and the ignition module. So I replaced the pick-up coil (for which you have to remove the entire ignition) and the problems are completely solved!! So check for corrosion under the distributor cap, which does seem to happen a lot if your Fiero is not being used regularly!||||reg||14
Z||000014||Patrick||04-24-2000||04:05 PM||||<BR>Glad to hear that you fixed your problem, Joe. However, this is an example of what I was stating earlier where there is a difference between what remedy works for the regular ignition system and what remedy works for the DIS system. I have the DIS system and therefore cannot try and do what worked for you.<P>I have some questions though, Joe. If the problem was caused by a corroded connection, why was there a need to replace the pick-up coil? Could you not have simply cleaned up the connection? Did the corroded connection somehow lead to the pick-up coil breaking down?<p>[This message has been edited by Patrick (edited 04-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||mshill||04-24-2000||06:15 PM||||Patrick,  I can answer your question about having to replace the pickup coil just because the connection was corroded. <P>The pickup coil is sandwiched between two plastic discs and the bottom disc has a nubbie on it that fits into a hole in the distributor base for correct positioning.  Well, needless to say that the plastic deteriorates with the heat generated in the engine compartment causing the plastic nubbie to break off and the coil can move freely.  This is a bad thing!  I tried to clean the green corrosion off of the pickup coil connectors and the plastic casing just crumbled, hence the coil gets replaced.  <P>Basically, if the connections are corroded, the coil WILL FAIL if you try to just clean the connections.||||reg||1
Z||000016||joeformula88||04-25-2000||02:53 AM||||Thanks MSHILL,<BR>That would have been my answer exactly. Even if the plastic thingy wouldn't break off, it's close to impossible to correctly clean the connections. I'm sorry for Patrick that this fix doesn't work for the DIS system. Good luck fixing yours! ||||reg||12
Z||000017||Patrick||04-25-2000||03:51 AM||||<BR>Thanks for the information guys. Although the DIS system is different, there are of course components that can go screwy with this system also. They're just not located in a distributor is all.<P>Well, I haven't really had a chance to test out the Fiero since I put a lower temperature thermostat in (because of my starter messing up), but the car will be getting a workout tomorrow. I'll be reporting any improvements (if any) from using the 180 thermostat.||||reg||14
Z||000018||Alex4mula||04-25-2000||12:49 PM||||I have an 88 Formula 5 speed. Had have it since new. From my 12 years experience I have learned to allways keep a look inside the distributor and have all those parts clean and new. Two things that can left you stranded anywhere is a bad pick-up coil or module. I replace my pick-up coil every 12-15K miles and clean everything and allways carry a spare module. At that time also replace the dielectric grease under the module. Since following that procedure have never got stranded for that. Now getting more related to your problem I think you have not targeted where really should be the root cause. The failure you have seems random and heat related. That may point me to the module. I would change the pickup coil first because it is cheaper ($14). By 60K miles if it has not been changed is already time to do it. Before changing the module get it to an auto parts store and get it tested. They do it for free. I would replace that one and keep the other as spare if you have the money. <BR>Another thing that you mention still have me in doubt. You said that it dies sometimes when stoping. When I had that problem it was usually related to the TPS. On a final comment two month ago my car was hard to start hot but it cranked slowly. The problem was that the starter selenoid did not worked ok when hot (from engine heat). Replaced the starter and problem ended. Just a note but I think your problem still is in your ignition components. Hope this helps||||reg||1
Z||000019||terryk||04-25-2000||07:01 PM||||Did you disconnect the cold start injector? The 660 loves fuel when it's cold, so it will start fine. Then when it's hot, it hates the CSI being on.<P>Makes cruise goofy too.||||reg||1
